# A nested definition whose tail begins with a known method calls nothing, as a unit item does (Q58; the author,
# 2026-10-05 and 2026-10-06: a call needs its Frame): `C: tick 7` in S defines C, and running S prints nothing.  It
# was read as tick's value and refused "internal: an own declaration has no physical field".  report prints DONE (a
# C door keeps it native when the methods are walked) and gives 7.  The program says only DONE.  Entry 7.
fn: tick () int
    c.printf: "tick\n"
    return: 1
end: tick
S:
    C: tick 7
end: S
fn: report () int
    c.printf: "DONE\n"
    return: 7
end: report
S
sendMessage: exit(exit_code: report(); stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
